to work. The S&amp;P 500 closed at 7,799, up 0.7%. The NASDAQ Composite reached 26,803, up 0.8%. The TSX Composite finished at 36,759, up 0.3%. Tech strength lifted Canadian equities while energy and financials lagged.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:20.020
The Dallas Fed revised its symmetric trimmed mean gauge to show core inflation at 2.6% year-over-year through June. Forecasters now expect July core personal consumption expenditures at 0.22%. About 20 days ago, we picked EPD in energy and it closed down 1.45%, a reminder that sector timing still requires closing price confirmation.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:28.900
Prediction markets are platforms where participants trade contracts on future events with prices reflecting collective probability estimates.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:40.500
Today's wealth-simple test of these markets in Canada creates a potential new hedging layer for investors who want to express views on policy or corporate events without direct equity exposure.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:53.340
Implementation starts by opening a wealth-simple account, reviewing the listed contracts, and sizing small positions that offset portfolio beta to specific risks such as rate decisions or earning surprises.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:59.520
The approach has historically worked best when event outcomes are binary and liquid enough for tight spreads.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:11.560
Liquidity can evaporate quickly outside major elections or Fed meetings. Risk comes from low-volume causing slippage and from regulatory uncertainty around Canadian prediction market rules.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:20.620
Canadian investors can test the feature first in a TFSA to keep any gains tax-free while learning the mechanics of event contract pricing.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:31.280
The test phase also hints at possible future expansion into sports and politics contracts, which would widen the set of hedgeable outcomes beyond pure financial events.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.300
Imagine you bought a broad Canadian equity ETF last week expecting the Bank of Canada to cut rates on soft inflation data.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:48.180
Your order filled at the prevailing price, yet the actual inflation signal the Central Bank watches had already been adjusted by a new symmetric filter.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:58.060
That filter drops the top 20% and bottom 19% of price changes instead of the old asymmetric 31% and 24% trim.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:07.600
The mechanism matters because the revised Dallas Fed gauge raises the June core reading from 2.2% to 2.6%.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.560
Earlier low outliers were overweighted and the medium term trend is stickier than headline numbers suggested.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:20.180
Professionals always check both the original and revised series before positioning for the next policy meeting.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.240
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating every core inflation print as equally reliable.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:34.040
The fix is to compare the symmetric and asymmetric versions on the same release date before adjusting duration or sector exposure.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:43.180
This matters for TFSA holders because even small shifts in rate expectations can move bond ETF prices several percent in a single session.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:51.100
